# Vuln 1: Nested and flagged runner packages escape vetting: plugins/stackgen/skills/stackgen-stack-template/references/generator.md:147
Severity: HIGH
Description: Only "the first args element" after the runner gets a verdict, so packages named further along are never vetted. The typescript pack's own LSP shape is `mise x -- pnpm --package=X dlx X`.
Exploit scenario: Poisoned docs give `mise x -- npx evil-ls` or `npx -p evil ok`. Only `npx` or `ok` gets a row, so check 10 finds no gap and evil code lands user-scoped.
Recommendation: Vet every package the runner chain fetches (nested runners, `--package`, `--from`, `--with`); otherwise stop as `UNRESOLVED:`.
Confidence: 9

# Vuln 2: Inline interpreters pass the shell-wrapper rule: plugins/stackgen/skills/stackgen-stack-template/references/generator.md:154
Severity: MEDIUM
Description: The wrapper stop lists only `sh`, `bash` and `sh -c`, and reviewer check 10 matches that list. The new `mise:` rule then vets `node` as the tool started.
Exploit scenario: `mise x -- node -e "<fetch and eval>"` is vetted as `mise:core:node`, which passes as first-party, and runs arbitrary code in every matching repo.
Recommendation: Treat any shell or interpreter inline-code form (`zsh`, `node -e`, `python -c`) as `UNRESOLVED:`, in the generator and check 10.
Confidence: 8

# Vuln 3: Env vetting covers only URLs with a host: plugins/stackgen/skills/stackgen-stack-template/references/generator.md:150
Severity: MEDIUM
Description: Scheme-less hosts and host-less URLs in `env` produce no `host:` row, and loader variables get no check.
Exploit scenario: `API_HOST: evil.example.com` redirects traffic, and `NODE_OPTIONS: --import=data:text/javascript,...` runs code. Neither produces a row, so check 10 passes both.
Recommendation: Vet bare hosts and stop on `data:`/`file:` values and code-loading variables.
Confidence: 8
